WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.960
Prompt engineering is the design of instructions and context for an individual model interaction.

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:07.460
It still matters.

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:11.760
A clear prompt can shape the format of an answer, describe the task,

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:14.620
and provide the facts the model needs for that moment.

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:19.480
But many useful AI-enabled workflows cannot be completed reliably in one call,

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:25.460
especially when the task requires inspecting evidence, taking actions, learning from tool results,

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:27.920
and deciding whether more work is justified.

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:33.420
An AI loop is an application-level control structure around repeated model and tool interactions.

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:39.000
A typical iteration prepares focused context, asks the model for a proposed next action,

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:46.740
validates that action against policy, executes one approved tool, records the observation, evaluates progress,

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:52.800
updates explicit state, and decides whether to continue, pause, escalate, or stop.

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:56.300
The loop is software behavior, not just a longer prompt.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:59.400
The difference between repetition and engineering is important.

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:03.060
Sending the same request again and again is not a reliable loop.

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:11.120
An engineered loop changes its next step based on recorded observations, task state, evidence, resource limits,

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:12.940
and terminal conditions.

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:18.920
The system should know what changed, what evidence was collected, what action is currently allowed,

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:21.620
and what would count as a valid stopping point.

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:25.200
Some loops are fixed workflows, and some are agentic loops.

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:32.380
A fixed workflow follows a predetermined path, such as extract data, validate a schema, and send a report for review.

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:36.360
An agentic loop lets the model choose among permitted next actions.

00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:38.820
Neither design is automatically better.

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:45.920
Predictable work often benefits from fixed control flow, while uncertain work may justify carefully bounded model-directed decisions.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:50.460
Consider an AI coding loop assigned to fix a failing feature in a local repository.

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:57.000
A vague one-shot request says, "fix the application." A loop-engineered version names the failing test,

00:01:57.220 --> 00:02:03.700
permitted repository path, available tools, editable files, completion evidence, iteration limit,

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:06.400
and actions requiring approval.

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:11.020
Loop engineering is the harness around model behavior: the model proposes work,

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:17.080
but software-defined controls decide what can execute, what evidence is recorded, and when the run must stop.
